  <p>A deload is a planned lighter week. Strength coach Jordan Hoppel keeps his definition simple, and so is his rule for knowing when to use one.</p>

  <h2>How to run a deload</h2>
  <p>Drop the volume. Keep intensity generally the same, but cut volume in half. So if you normally do 6 sets of a lift, do 3, at around the same weight.</p>

  <Media type="image" alt="Side-by-side comparison of a normal training week and a deload week showing halved sets" caption="Same weights, half the sets." brief="Graphic: normal week vs. deload week (sets halved, load unchanged)." />

  <h2>How to know you need one</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You report poor sleep.</li>
    <li>Training feels like a grind.</li>
    <li>You feel worn out.</li>
    <li>Your numbers trend downward, or haven't trended upward in a very long time.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In maintenance or in-season phases the goal isn't getting stronger, just recovering from game to game.</p>

  <JordanQuote>Training frequency is based on how quickly you can recover; it cannot outpace recovery if you want continued quality progress.</JordanQuote>

  <h2>Is soreness the signal?</h2>
  <p>Not really. Soreness is a response a muscle experiences after being placed under stress. It's not necessarily a predictor of a good workout; it's a byproduct of workouts in general.</p>

  <h2>Deload vs. a real plateau</h2>
  <p>If you're recovered and the weights still don't move, that's a training plateau, not a recovery problem. See <a href="/progressive-overload-explained/">progressive overload explained</a> for what to change.</p>
